{
  "description": "Capturing the registering frame's bindings does not put a handler into that procedure's control flow (spec/execution-model.md:657-664): a handler invocation is a separate, deferred instruction, so `return` inside a handler block is outside any procedure and raises `ol-return-outside-proc` (spec/error-model.md:116) \"even when the handler was registered inside a `define`\". The spec gives two reasons and this fixture is the second one — \"one firing after it returned would have no procedure to leave\": `setup` has already returned by the time the `every` fires from the top-level `wait`, so there is no frame the signal could be consumed as the result of. The existing `interaction-events/when/when-return-in-handler-inside-proc` covers the first reason, a handler firing while the registering call is still on the stack; that shape cannot show this one. The `return` trace event is still emitted by the statement before the handler boundary reclassifies the escaping signal. Measured during the review gate: removing the handler-level conversion alone does NOT change this program's output, because a signal escaping to the top level is converted there under the same code — the implementation this fixture discriminates against is one that SWALLOWS a deferred `return`, which produces no diagnostic at all.",
